": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_16", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "Structurally there is a short static path from 1010 to 883: 1010\u2192189 (step_11), 189\u2192704 (step_09), 704\u2192883 (step_10); 1010 and 883 are not directly connected (step_14). However, every one of these hops is carried by a single event (n_events = 1 each), and node 1010 has only 6 total events while 883 has 12 (step_02), so the cascade would have to rest on exactly three unrepeated interactions. Critically, none of the tools available in this session return event timestamps, so I could not verify that these three events occur in increasing time order, that they all fall inside [51317, 89717], or that consecutive hops are separated by at most 600 time units \u2014 the defining requirements of a time-respecting cascade. The alternative explanations were checked and are live: the static path passes through/near very high-degree hubs (158 has 1029 events and 363 out-neighbours, 704 has 60 in-neighbours; steps 12, 13, 16), and in a graph of 16325 events over 15458 distinct edges with a repeat ratio of only 0.053 (step_01), a 3-hop static connection between two arbitrary nodes is close to expected by chance. Static reachability plus hub popularity therefore cannot substitute for the missing temporal ordering evidence, so the correct answer is insufficient rather than present or absent."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8980b60f\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1010, 883, 189, 704, 158],\n  \"window\": [51317, 89717],\n  \"confidence\": 0.42,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Structurally there is a short static path from 1010 to 883: 1010\u2192189 (step_11), 189\u2192704 (step_09), 704\u2192883 (step_10); 1010 and 883 are not directly connected (step_14). However, every one of these hops is carried by a single event (n_events = 1 each), and node 1010 has only 6 total events while 883 has 12 (step_02), so the cascade would have to rest on exactly three unrepeated interactions. Critically, none of the tools available in this session return event timestamps, so I could not verify that these three events occur in increasing time order, that they all fall inside [51317, 89717], or that consecutive hops are separated by at most 600 time units \u2014 the defining requirements of a time-respecting cascade. The alternative explanations were checked and are live: the static path passes through/near very high-degree hubs (158 has 1029 events and 363 out-neighbours, 704 has 60 in-neighbours; steps 12, 13, 16), and in a graph of 16325 events over 15458 distinct edges with a repeat ratio of only 0.053 (step_01), a 3-hop static connection between two arbitrary nodes is close to expected by chance. Static reachability plus hub popularity therefore cannot substitute for the missing temporal ordering evidence, so the correct answer is insufficient rather than present or absent.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 36420, "total_output_tokens": 3939, "total_tokens": 40359, "total_latency_ms": 58286, "llm_calls": 8, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}